WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.720
The oral mechanism examination asks whether structure or function contributes to communication.

2
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.000
Observe posture and natural rest before giving commands.

3
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:13.960
Then compare movement, symmetry, range, coordination and endurance.

4
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.760
Standard precautions apply across settings.

5
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:21.840
Gloves, adequate lighting and appropriate examination tools support clear observation.

6
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.420
Record what you see and how it affects the task.

7
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.920
Pucker and smile examine lip range and symmetry.

8
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.380
Puffing and holding cheek air probes lip closure

9
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.620
and pressure, with attention to nasal emission.

10
00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.160
Tongue movements examine excursion, range, symmetry and strength.

11
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:43.080
Sustaining ah allows observation of velar movement and the uvula, alongside nasality.

12
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.140
The point of Form six dash one is to understand why

13
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.080
the task is used, not recite commands without a purpose.

14
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.060
Stability supports a position.

15
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.080
Mobility moves through range.

16
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.420
Dissociation moves one part independently.

17
00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.880
Grading controls speed, force and range.

18
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.140
A jaw moving during a tongue task may

19
00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.340
be compensation rather than independent tongue control.

20
00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.740
The assigned video distinguishes horizontal overjet from vertical overbite.

21
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.700
Its submucous cleft warning combines a bifid uvula,

22
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.700
translucent midline zone and posterior hard-palate notch.

23
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.000
One sign alone does not establish the diagnosis.

24
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:23.580
Alternating motion rate repeats one syllable; sequential motion

25
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.280
rate changes syllables, such as puh tuh kuh.

26
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:31.240
Observe accuracy, rhythm, consistency and coordination as well as rate.

27
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.700
Adequate single-syllable repetitions with difficulty sequencing raises a motor-planning

28
00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:39.340
question; it is not a stand-alone CAS diagnosis.

29
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.020
Relate each observation to speech, resonance, feeding or

30
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.708
airway function and the need for additional assessment.
